          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"shakespeare-king-lear-cf26d564136c4590\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Goneril’s closing “I love you” is a direct, hyperbolic avowal that presents extreme filial love as true. Its strategic and possibly deceptive use does not reduce its truth-conditional mode. The speech makes no substantial commitment or relational undertaking, and its ornate intensity supplies no evidence that the words are a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The ceremonial setting gives the profession relational consequences, but those consequences and its likely strategic purpose do not independently establish performative force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Goneril directly addresses Lear in a public love contest; the highlighted closing declaration repeats and culminates her extended profession of filial love.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Lear has explicitly invited his daughters to compete in professing love, with larger territorial rewards attached to the strongest answer. Goneril responds with elaborately patterned hyperbole, claiming that her love exceeds words, eyesight, liberty, and life. The incentivised public setting and Cordelia’s skeptical asides frame the declaration as calculated or unreliable, but deception is compatible with a strongly truth-conditional utterance type. Nothing in the passage presents the words as involuntarily blurted or affectively forced.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter” and the closing “Beyond all manner of so much I love you.”\",\"supports\":\"The utterance explicitly presents Goneril’s purported loving state and its extraordinary degree as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Lear asks which daughter “doth love us most” so that he may extend his “largest bounty.”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration occurs in an incentivised public competition, supporting a strategic contextual interpretation without changing its avowal-like core force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Goneril’s speech consists of comparative claims about the magnitude and value of her love, without a promise to act, remain, serve, or assume a new obligation.\",\"supports\":\"This weighs against substantial performative or undertaking force.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Cordelia responds aside, “What shall Cordelia speak? Love, and be silent,” and later contrasts her sisters’ claims with her own bounded filial love.\",\"supports\":\"The immediate dramatic framing casts doubt on Goneril’s sincerity and highlights the rhetorical excess of her profession.\",\"confidence\":0.91},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The declaration is delivered as a sustained, formally constructed sequence of hyperboles rather than being narrated as an unpremeditated verbal eruption.\",\"supports\":\"Intensity and rhetoric alone do not establish expressive/reflexive force; the passage supplies no positive evidence for E.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the declaration as an avowal or asserted profession, while its strategic setting, probable insincerity, and rhetorical excess are adequately handled as context. No residual core-force dimension requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The judgement concerns Goneril’s highlighted closing “I love you,” interpreted as the culmination of her full speech.\"}"
        }
